{
  "updated_at": "2026-09-18 17:12 JST",
  "total": 50,
  "toyota_summary": "China's auto parts imports into the EU hit 5 billion euros in the first half of 2026, up 23 percent year-over-year, marking a significant shift in where European carmakers source components. At the same time, Chinese automakers exported over 1 million vehicles for a third consecutive month, signaling sustained momentum in global market share gains. EU suppliers are losing pricing power and volume to Chinese competitors across batteries, semiconductors, and traditional powertrain parts.\n\nFor European and other global automakers, this creates a two-front squeeze. Rising exposure to Chinese parts raises geopolitical risk around supply chain security and tariff retaliation, while also pressuring margins as Chinese suppliers undercut on price. The faster Chinese makers move upmarket—some now competing in premium segments—the more direct the threat becomes to legacy carmakers' core business.\n\nWatch whether the EU's Industrial Accelerator Act will impose stricter local-content rules or tariffs on Chinese parts.",
